::CW_U05_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Draft excerpt: I waited outside the rehearsal room. The music stopped. I raised my hand to knock, then lowered it. The writer wants a poem that emphasizes hesitation. Which format choice best fits?{
=A. Use line breaks around "raised my hand" and "lowered it" so the reader pauses with the speaker.#Correct. The format choice supports pacing and meaning.
~B. Put the entire draft in one long line.#Incorrect. One long line may reduce the pause that shows hesitation.
~C. Add random spaces between every word.#Incorrect. Random spacing distracts the reader and does not clearly support meaning.
~D. Remove all punctuation and format choices.#Incorrect. Removing all control can make the piece harder to read.
}

::CW_U05_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Draft excerpt: "Come in" Mara said but she did not open the door. Which prose-story revision best improves publication quality?{
~A. come in mara said but she did not open the door#Incorrect. This removes punctuation and capitalization, which weakens readability.
~B. COME IN MARA SAID BUT SHE DID NOT OPEN THE DOORfor this item#Incorrect. All capitals do not create clear prose formatting.
~C. Come in. Mara. Door. Said.#Incorrect. This fragments the sentence and makes meaning less clear.
=D. "Come in," Mara said, but she did not open the door.#Correct. The punctuation and sentence control make the dialogue clear.
}

::CW_U05_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Script context: Mara speaks while keeping one hand on the door. Which format best fits a script?{
~A. Mara was nervous in a paragraph with no speaker label.#Incorrect. A script needs clear speaker labels for performance reading.
=B. MARA (keeping one hand on the door): Do not come in yet.#Correct. The character label and stage direction support script format and meaning.
~C. Do not come in yet, someone said somewhere.#Incorrect. This hides the speaker and does not use script format.
~D. A decorative title with no dialogue.#Incorrect. Decoration does not replace format features needed for a script.
}

::CW_U05_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A writer says, "I changed the spacing because it looked creative." What is the best revision of that explanation?{
~A. I changed the spacing because all poems need strange spacing.#Incorrect. This is a false rule and does not explain the effect.
~B. I changed the spacing because my friend told me to.#Incorrect. This does not explain how the format helps the reader.
=C. I placed the final phrase on its own line so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This names the format choice and explains the reader effect.
~D. I changed the spacing for no reason.#Incorrect. Publication choices should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Format check: A nonfiction reflection has one paragraph that combines three different moments. What should the writer check first?{
=A. Whether paragraph breaks would separate the moments and improve clarity.#Correct. Format-specific quality means the structure should help the reader understand the piece.
~B. Whether the title can be a random symbol.#Incorrect. The issue is organization and reader tracking, not decoration.
~C. Whether the piece can ignore paragraphing.#Incorrect. Paragraphing helps readers follow shifts in thought or time.
~D. Whether every sentence should be deleted.#Incorrect. Deleting everything is not a targeted format revision.
}
